1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,875
Please use Walker to explain what I must
understand when Claude

2
00:00:03,042 --> 00:00:04,375
Code builds my Godot game.

3
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:08,417
That is the question behind C S Y E
seventy-two seventy.

4
00:00:08,458 --> 00:00:10,083
I'm Liam, in for Professor Bear.

5
00:00:10,625 --> 00:00:13,625
This course is about directing and
evaluating virtual environments,

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,583
not collecting impressive-looking code you
cannot explain.

7
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:19,750
The scarce skill is not typing.

8
00:00:20,042 --> 00:00:22,750
It is judgment. Claude can help produce
the implementation.

9
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:26,333
You must decide what the game is trying to
do, recognize a plausible

10
00:00:26,333 --> 00:00:28,750
mistake, and explain why a revision is
better.

11
00:00:29,208 --> 00:00:32,125
We will follow one small platformer from a
vague request to

12
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:33,250
evidence you can defend.

13
00:00:34,208 --> 00:00:37,500
Separate the roles. You supply the intent
and acceptance criteria.

14
00:00:37,958 --> 00:00:41,500
Walker organizes the brief, build,
playtest, inspection, revision,

15
00:00:41,667 --> 00:00:44,833
and export cycle. Claude Code proposes and
edits code.

16
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:47,000
Godot runs the interactive world.

17
00:00:47,292 --> 00:00:50,500
An agent's confident description is not a
substitute for observing

18
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:51,083
that world.

19
00:00:51,958 --> 00:00:54,875
Here is the actual Walker Jumpman starter,
before a student's

20
00:00:54,875 --> 00:00:58,583
extension. This is an archived frame from
the existing project,

21
00:00:58,583 --> 00:01:01,958
not a proposed redesign. Establish that
baseline first.

22
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:05,292
If you never observe the original controls
and level, you will

23
00:01:05,292 --> 00:01:07,333
not know whether your changes preserved
them.

24
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:11,250
Godot supplies nodes, scenes, rendering,
physics, and the running

25
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:14,417
game. G D Script connects those pieces
with behavior.

26
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:16,875
The course uses the regular Godot engine.

27
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:20,458
The starter does not require C sharp or
the dot net edition.

28
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,417
Claude Code is the coding assistant; it is
not the engine.

29
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:27,208
The Walker loop has six moves: game brief,
build, playtest,

30
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:30,417
inspect, revise, export. Notice where
inspection sits.

31
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:33,708
Playing reveals an observation; inspection
traces its cause;

32
00:01:33,958 --> 00:01:37,083
revision changes the cause. Export
packages a version.

33
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:39,958
It does not retroactively prove that the
version was good.

34
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:43,833
These exercises assume working Claude Code
access through the

35
00:01:43,833 --> 00:01:46,875
course's Northeastern arrangements. Check
that access early

36
00:01:46,875 --> 00:01:48,792
and contact the instructor if it fails.

37
00:01:49,333 --> 00:01:51,958
Separate A P I credits are not a starter
prerequisite.

38
00:01:52,417 --> 00:01:56,000
Install the course-specified Godot
version, then open the starter's

39
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,167
project file rather than creating an
unrelated empty project.

40
00:02:00,042 --> 00:02:02,292
The course names five supervisory
capacities.

41
00:02:02,583 --> 00:02:05,333
Plausibility auditing asks whether the
result makes sense.

42
00:02:05,708 --> 00:02:07,625
Problem formulation defines the task.

43
00:02:07,917 --> 00:02:09,500
Tool orchestration orders the work.

44
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:12,583
Interpretive judgment gives the result
game-specific meaning.

45
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,667
Executive integration keeps the session
aimed at one goal.

46
00:02:16,125 --> 00:02:19,125
These are the course's vocabulary for
supervision, not five

47
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:20,250
buttons in Godot.

48
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,417
Problem formulation begins before
prompting.

49
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:25,750
Make the character better is not a
specification.

50
00:02:26,333 --> 00:02:28,958
Give the character a distinct silhouette
while preserving movement

51
00:02:28,958 --> 00:02:31,000
speed and collision behavior is closer.

52
00:02:31,583 --> 00:02:34,917
Name one concern, an invariant you will
preserve, and the files

53
00:02:34,917 --> 00:02:37,042
or systems the agent must leave alone.

54
00:02:37,958 --> 00:02:39,958
Tool orchestration is about dependencies.

55
00:02:40,542 --> 00:02:42,167
Inspect the existing player first.

56
00:02:42,375 --> 00:02:43,750
Change its visual identity next.

57
00:02:44,042 --> 00:02:45,208
Check the silhouette in motion.

58
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:47,875
Only then extend the level in a separate
change.

59
00:02:48,083 --> 00:02:51,583
If art, physics, camera behavior, and
geometry all move together,

60
00:02:51,875 --> 00:02:53,958
a regression has too many possible causes.

61
00:02:55,417 --> 00:02:57,500
Plausibility auditing starts with a
specific suspicion.

62
00:02:58,042 --> 00:03:00,667
This archived jump frame tells us where
the visible character

63
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:03,208
was. It does not prove that every landing
works.

64
00:03:03,417 --> 00:03:06,042
A new sprite could look centered while its
collision shape stays

65
00:03:06,042 --> 00:03:09,250
elsewhere. Ask what observation would
reveal that mismatch,

66
00:03:09,375 --> 00:03:09,958
then test it.

67
00:03:10,792 --> 00:03:13,333
Interpretive judgment supplies the meaning
of a result.

68
00:03:13,625 --> 00:03:15,375
A collision test can report contact.

69
00:03:15,875 --> 00:03:18,375
It cannot decide for your audience whether
the landing feels

70
00:03:18,375 --> 00:03:21,083
fair or the character reads clearly
against the background.

71
00:03:21,583 --> 00:03:23,042
Use machine checks as evidence.

72
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:26,125
Make the design judgment yourself, and
explain the tradeoff

73
00:03:26,125 --> 00:03:26,792
you accepted.

74
00:03:27,708 --> 00:03:30,375
Executive integration prevents the session
from drifting.

75
00:03:30,875 --> 00:03:33,708
The assignment is to change a character
and extend a playable

76
00:03:33,708 --> 00:03:37,125
level. A proposed inventory system might
be clever and completely

77
00:03:37,125 --> 00:03:40,875
irrelevant. Keep the brief visible, record
deferred ideas, and

78
00:03:40,875 --> 00:03:43,708
judge each new task against the goal
instead of the agent's

79
00:03:43,708 --> 00:03:44,333
enthusiasm.

80
00:03:45,333 --> 00:03:46,792
Now inspect the real starter.

81
00:03:47,167 --> 00:03:49,333
Player drawing and movement live in the
player script.

82
00:03:49,833 --> 00:03:51,833
Level geometry comes from a data file.

83
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:54,875
The session script builds and manages the
running world.

84
00:03:55,208 --> 00:03:57,875
That ownership map tells you where to ask
for a change and where

85
00:03:57,875 --> 00:03:59,583
to look when the result surprises you.

86
00:04:00,417 --> 00:04:03,167
The first change is a new character
identity, not just a new

87
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:05,917
color. Use the baseline as a comparison
reference.

88
00:04:06,292 --> 00:04:08,917
A student might change the silhouette,
face, or accessories

89
00:04:08,917 --> 00:04:10,625
while preserving the movement contract.

90
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,333
That redesigned character is proposed work
here.

91
00:04:13,875 --> 00:04:17,042
This film is not presenting an unbuilt
student solution as finished

92
00:04:17,042 --> 00:04:17,500
gameplay.

93
00:04:18,458 --> 00:04:21,125
In this source snapshot, the player's
collision rectangle is

94
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:22,875
eighteen by twenty-eight game units.

95
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:24,708
The drawing is a separate concern.

96
00:04:25,083 --> 00:04:27,625
Our schematic enlarges both so you can see
the difference.

97
00:04:28,125 --> 00:04:30,708
Changing the art does not automatically
change the collider.

98
00:04:31,083 --> 00:04:33,875
If you intentionally change collision,
update the specification

99
00:04:33,875 --> 00:04:35,042
and the tests too.

100
00:04:35,958 --> 00:04:38,542
For the first assignment, add at least two
new jump-and-landing

101
00:04:38,583 --> 00:04:41,083
opportunities and relocate the finish to
the new end.

102
00:04:41,542 --> 00:04:43,583
More floor is not automatically more play.

103
00:04:44,042 --> 00:04:47,083
Show the route, explain the choices it
asks of the player, and

104
00:04:47,083 --> 00:04:49,417
preserve a reachable start-to-finish
experience.

105
00:04:50,375 --> 00:04:53,250
This archived completion frame came from
scripted input.

106
00:04:53,625 --> 00:04:56,542
It demonstrates one successful route in
the recorded build.

107
00:04:56,875 --> 00:04:59,708
It is not a human playtest, not a
measurement of enjoyment,

108
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,167
and not evidence about your future
extension.

109
00:05:02,792 --> 00:05:05,542
Preserve that distinction when you
describe tests in your own

110
00:05:05,542 --> 00:05:06,042
submission.

111
00:05:06,917 --> 00:05:09,625
A larger level affects more than one
visible platform.

112
00:05:10,083 --> 00:05:12,958
The finish position, camera limits,
resource references, and

113
00:05:12,958 --> 00:05:15,167
runtime behavior may depend on other
values.

114
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:17,292
The course calls this the dangerous
middle.

115
00:05:17,750 --> 00:05:20,458
Follow the dependency links. Do not
approve a change merely

116
00:05:20,458 --> 00:05:22,625
because the file you edited looks
reasonable.

117
00:05:23,625 --> 00:05:25,917
Before the next build, write a prediction
that can fail.

118
00:05:26,417 --> 00:05:29,167
After extending the level, I can land on
both new platforms,

119
00:05:29,417 --> 00:05:32,542
reach the new finish, die, and restart at
the original spawn.

120
00:05:33,042 --> 00:05:34,875
Record the observed result for each part.

121
00:05:35,292 --> 00:05:37,958
A claim becomes useful when you know what
would contradict it.

122
00:05:38,792 --> 00:05:40,625
Suppose the second new landing fails.

123
00:05:40,875 --> 00:05:43,750
That is a constructed teaching example,
not a new test run.

124
00:05:44,125 --> 00:05:46,833
Compare the geometry with the movement you
promised to preserve.

125
00:05:47,333 --> 00:05:50,708
Adjust one cause, rerun the failing case,
then repeat the baseline

126
00:05:50,708 --> 00:05:53,875
checks. Quietly weakening the test would
hide the problem instead

127
00:05:53,875 --> 00:05:54,500
of fixing it.

128
00:05:55,375 --> 00:05:58,375
In week two, the same supervisory pattern
moves to game art.

129
00:05:58,667 --> 00:06:01,583
Use tools you can access, compare
candidates, record prompts

130
00:06:01,875 --> 00:06:04,583
and provenance, and inspect the imported
result at game scale.

131
00:06:05,125 --> 00:06:08,333
A beautiful isolated image can become an
unreadable character.

132
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:11,375
A paid image subscription is not the
learning objective.

133
00:06:12,292 --> 00:06:15,250
Week three adds Blender through the
community Blender M C P

134
00:06:15,292 --> 00:06:18,625
integration. A generated mesh is an
intermediate artifact.

135
00:06:19,042 --> 00:06:22,917
Inspect its geometry, scale, materials,
and exported form, then

136
00:06:22,917 --> 00:06:26,708
verify it inside Godot. A correct-looking
Blender viewport does

137
00:06:26,708 --> 00:06:29,833
not prove that the imported asset behaves
correctly in the game.

138
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:33,333
The middle of the course connects scenes,
collision, and physics

139
00:06:33,333 --> 00:06:36,375
to the game design document, then to
shaders and materials.

140
00:06:36,875 --> 00:06:39,458
Specify what should happen before asking
for code.

141
00:06:39,875 --> 00:06:42,833
A shader compiling establishes one kind of
conformance.

142
00:06:43,083 --> 00:06:45,833
Compare controlled inputs and visible
output before calling

143
00:06:45,833 --> 00:06:46,833
its appearance correct.

144
00:06:47,792 --> 00:06:51,292
Particles, animation, interaction, and
audio unfold over time.

145
00:06:51,542 --> 00:06:54,417
A still screenshot cannot show whether an
effect starts too

146
00:06:54,417 --> 00:06:57,667
early, an animation transition pops, or a
sound repeats after

147
00:06:57,667 --> 00:07:00,833
restart. Trace the event, the state
change, and the visible

148
00:07:00,833 --> 00:07:03,917
or audible response. Then test that
sequence in the running

149
00:07:03,917 --> 00:07:04,292
scene.

150
00:07:05,208 --> 00:07:08,917
Later work adds profiling, analytics,
navigation, behavior trees,

151
00:07:09,167 --> 00:07:12,125
procedural content, and appropriately
scoped networking, mobile,

152
00:07:12,375 --> 00:07:15,750
or extended-reality constraints. These are
still Walker and

153
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:19,500
Godot problems. Measure a baseline,
justify a focused change,

154
00:07:19,833 --> 00:07:22,500
and integrate the result into projects you
can reproduce and

155
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:22,800
defend.

156
00:07:23,750 --> 00:07:26,542
Modules map one-to-one to companion-book
chapters, released

157
00:07:26,542 --> 00:07:27,708
one or two per week.

158
00:07:28,083 --> 00:07:30,750
Assignments follow a separate clock: one
hundred points every

159
00:07:30,917 --> 00:07:34,000
ten days. There is an individual project
and a group project.

160
00:07:34,458 --> 00:07:37,833
Canvas supplies the deadlines and project
milestones; this overview

161
00:07:37,833 --> 00:07:40,167
does not invent dates or project
weightings.

162
00:07:40,958 --> 00:07:44,000
Every assignment also includes a Brutalist
Godot explainer.

163
00:07:44,375 --> 00:07:47,458
Use a walkthrough to show behavior, a
game-development explainer

164
00:07:47,458 --> 00:07:50,917
to connect it to implementation, or a
game-design-document explainer

165
00:07:50,917 --> 00:07:52,375
when that is the assignment's focus.

166
00:07:53,083 --> 00:07:55,667
Selected films become class discussion
material.

167
00:07:56,125 --> 00:07:59,250
Narration should explain actual evidence,
not promise footage

168
00:07:59,250 --> 00:08:00,250
you never recorded.

169
00:08:01,125 --> 00:08:03,125
Predict, build it, use it, ship it,
verify.

170
00:08:03,417 --> 00:08:05,042
Predict the result before prompting.

171
00:08:05,208 --> 00:08:07,417
Build one focused change. Use it in the
game.

172
00:08:07,750 --> 00:08:09,750
Ship a reproducible version and
explanation.

173
00:08:10,125 --> 00:08:11,708
Verify the claims against evidence.

174
00:08:12,333 --> 00:08:15,042
This learning loop and Walker's production
loop reinforce each

175
00:08:15,042 --> 00:08:17,750
other; neither means press generate and
trust the result.

176
00:08:18,667 --> 00:08:21,417
In your sources and Frictional log, name
what you contributed

177
00:08:21,417 --> 00:08:22,917
and what the A I contributed.

178
00:08:23,292 --> 00:08:26,125
Include the wrong turns, the evidence that
changed your mind,

179
00:08:26,125 --> 00:08:29,583
and what remains unverified. The GitHub
version and the Canvas

180
00:08:29,583 --> 00:08:33,083
submission must match. A polished film
cannot replace an honest

181
00:08:33,083 --> 00:08:33,875
account of the work.

182
00:08:34,750 --> 00:08:37,333
A failed run can teach more than a
confident summary.

183
00:08:37,792 --> 00:08:40,250
Here the archive captures the starter's
failure state.

184
00:08:40,583 --> 00:08:43,125
In your own work, inspect what triggered
failure and whether

185
00:08:43,125 --> 00:08:46,667
recovery behaves as specified. Then ask
the human question:

186
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,875
does this teach the player fairly, or
merely satisfy a machine

187
00:08:49,875 --> 00:08:50,250
check?

188
00:08:51,083 --> 00:08:54,208
The agent can help propose acceptance
tests, analyze traces,

189
00:08:54,458 --> 00:08:57,167
and suggest revisions. Those are useful
contributions.

190
00:08:57,583 --> 00:09:00,208
It still does not inherit your
responsibility for choosing the

191
00:09:00,208 --> 00:09:01,583
goal or defending the result.

192
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,708
Conformance asks whether the system meets
stated checks.

193
00:09:05,042 --> 00:09:07,833
Adequacy asks whether those checks and
that experience serve

194
00:09:07,833 --> 00:09:08,875
the intended player.

195
00:09:09,792 --> 00:09:12,833
The verdict: this is a course in
supervising interactive systems.

196
00:09:13,167 --> 00:09:16,167
Learn the subsystems well enough to
specify behavior and catch

197
00:09:16,167 --> 00:09:19,750
plausible errors. Use Claude Code to
accelerate implementation.

198
00:09:20,250 --> 00:09:21,833
Use Godot to observe the result.

199
00:09:22,292 --> 00:09:25,667
Use the five capacities to explain the
decisions that no generated

200
00:09:25,667 --> 00:09:27,125
code listing can make for you.

201
00:09:27,958 --> 00:09:31,042
Your turn. Please use Walker to inspect
Jumpman, preserve its

202
00:09:31,042 --> 00:09:33,708
movement, and propose a new character and
two new landings.

203
00:09:34,125 --> 00:09:36,958
Start with that prompt, then review the
proposed changes before

204
00:09:36,958 --> 00:09:40,083
accepting them. Your evidence should show
the baseline, the

205
00:09:40,083 --> 00:09:43,583
extension, a failure and restart, and one
revision you can explain.
